WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.991 --> 00:00:07.908
(orchestral music)

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.820
- [Narrator] Japadog is a portmanteau;

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:18.230
and for those of you that don't
know what a portmanteau is,

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:22.240
a portmanteau is when you
combine two distinct word forms.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.540
In this case, it's Japan and hot dog.

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:27.413
But this is not a story about words.

00:00:28.536 --> 00:00:30.620
This, my friends, is a
story about two cultures

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.500
coming together via hot dog.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.083
(upbeat music)

00:01:18.465 --> 00:01:19.830
- [Narrator] On the menu, you'll find dogs

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:22.350
with an assortment of
traditional Japanese flavors,

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.703
such as grated radish,
bonito flakes, and nori.

00:02:29.253 --> 00:02:32.170
(orchestral music)

00:02:39.743 --> 00:02:40.860
- [Narrator] There are
countless Chinatowns all over

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.580
the world, and New York
boasts one of the most famous

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:46.080
full of cheap produce, fish
markets, and, of course,

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:47.463
a plethora of Chinese food.

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:50.130
But, if you hop on the 1 train,

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:51.300
take it to the Upper West Side,

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.310
you'll find a Chinese
restaurant like no other,

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:55.947
where half the menu is something else.

00:02:55.947 --> 00:02:58.644
- [Male] Chuletta,
chicharones, ropa vieja,

00:02:58.644 --> 00:03:01.080
tostones, arroz y frijol.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:03.270
- [Narrator] This is La Caridad 78,

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:05.926
New York's last Cuban Chinese restaurant.

00:03:05.926 --> 00:03:09.819
(speaking foreign language)

00:03:09.819 --> 00:03:13.486
(speaking foreign language)

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:17.090
- [Narrator] How did this
unlikely culinary duo come to be?

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:20.202
That's a great question: Enter, Sam Lee.

00:03:20.202 --> 00:03:25.202
- Sorry, it was my wife.

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:33.510
- [Narrator] Hundreds of
thousands of Chinese came

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:36.250
to Cuba in the 19th
and early 20th century,

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:38.620
establishing a strong
presence on the island;

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:40.100
but when Castro came to power,

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:42.530
most Chinese immigrants,
like Sam's father left—

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:44.123
many of them coming to the U.S.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:47.533
And thus, Cuban Chinese food was born.

00:04:10.098 --> 00:04:14.598
- [Narrator] So are there
any Cuban-born workers left?

00:04:16.931 --> 00:04:20.598
(speaking foreign language)

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:37.600
- [Narrator] While Cuban
Chinese culture is fading,

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:40.736
La Caridad is a snapshot
of a strange moment in time

00:04:40.736 --> 00:04:43.723
where two vastly different
cultures merged together.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:08.917
(orchestral music)

00:05:10.614 --> 00:05:13.197
(upbeat music)

00:07:43.551 --> 00:07:46.468
(orchestral music)

00:09:52.833 --> 00:09:56.000
(upbeat piano music)

00:09:58.717 --> 00:10:00.410
- [Male] You're in Mississippi,

00:10:00.410 --> 00:10:02.443
and everywhere you go, tamales.

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:06.350
Those magical little snacks
of cornflour and meat

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:08.370
packed into a corn husk.

00:10:08.370 --> 00:10:10.593
Tamales, tamales, tamales.

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:14.740
So if you've ever wondered
what Mississippi tastes like,

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:17.293
it tastes like me, the tamale.

00:10:22.870 --> 00:10:26.070
My journey starts almost
a hundred years ago.

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:29.090
It's 1930, and Mexican migrants

00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:30.640
are tricklin' into Mississippi.

00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:34.860
They bring along a taste of home,

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:37.120
their version of the sandwich:

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:38.973
Meet the tamale.

00:10:41.334 --> 00:10:43.833
- My great-grandfather
came here from Mexico City,

00:10:43.833 --> 00:10:46.920
jumped the trains,
ended up in Mississippi.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:49.770
He decided to make his
family's homemade hand-rolled

00:10:49.770 --> 00:10:53.000
hot tamales, and in 1939,
opened up this place

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:54.403
and started selling tamales.

00:10:56.230 --> 00:10:57.820
You have to understand,
this is a family thing.

00:10:57.820 --> 00:10:59.590
I'm the fourth generation.

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:01.220
These same generational
people who have been coming

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:04.025
to the Big Apple Inn for the last 78 years

00:11:04.025 --> 00:11:04.858
are also generational.

00:11:04.858 --> 00:11:07.000
When they eat a tamale,
they always have a story.

00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:08.500
My daddy used to bring me here,

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:10.080
his daddy had the same kind of story,

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:12.490
his daddy had the same kind of story.

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:16.953
Tamales caught on here, and it
became a staple in the city.

00:11:19.690 --> 00:11:21.440
- [Male] So there I am, winnin' over

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:23.740
the Mississippi natives,
and as I make my way up

00:11:23.740 --> 00:11:26.800
into the Delta, my location may change,

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:28.390
but little did I know that the tradition

00:11:28.390 --> 00:11:31.870
of family most certainly would not.

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:34.700
Just like Geno and his
grandfather at the Big Apple Inn,

00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:37.130
I bring the Scott family together—

00:11:37.130 --> 00:11:39.600
a family who has gone on to be known

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:42.383
as the first family of
tamales in Mississippi.

00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:47.020
- In the early '50s, my father bought

00:11:47.020 --> 00:11:49.313
the recipe from a Mexican guy.

00:11:50.170 --> 00:11:52.370
My mother and father taught me,

00:11:52.370 --> 00:11:55.310
my sisters and my niece,
all of us together,

00:11:55.310 --> 00:11:59.373
we're makin' hot tamale, we
just one big hot tamale family.

00:12:00.530 --> 00:12:03.500
In a business that's family oriented,

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:05.583
I think it'll last longer.

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:10.800
- [Male] Much like the Scott family,

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:14.760
the Doe family is made up of
a long line of tamale makers.

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:17.050
Doe's Eat Place is a landmark restaurant

00:12:17.050 --> 00:12:20.319
that helped propel me
to become the backbone

00:12:20.319 --> 00:12:22.023
of Mississippi cuisine I've become today.

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:27.310
- My daddy started this
restaurant in 1941 with my mother.

00:12:27.310 --> 00:12:29.500
We've been here 76 years.

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:33.340
Momma had a sister that was livin' here,

00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:35.410
and Daddy had a couple,
two or three sisters,

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:38.793
that were livin' here, so they
all came and rolled tamales.

00:12:40.070 --> 00:12:44.227
A lot of people ask us, "How
did tamales get prevalent

00:12:44.227 --> 00:12:45.850
"in the belt?"

00:12:45.850 --> 00:12:47.500
It's been family.

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:49.100
You know, family's been here all the time.

00:12:50.840 --> 00:12:54.100
- [Male] As tamales make
their way from Mexican culture

00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:56.250
to African American communities

00:12:56.250 --> 00:12:58.570
and onto Mississippi's white population,

00:12:58.570 --> 00:13:02.393
there's one thing that
remained a constant, family,

00:13:03.342 --> 00:13:04.992
the most southern concept of all.

00:13:06.439 --> 00:13:08.939
(light music)

